rules_version = '2';

// Deny everything, on purpose.
//
// The registration portal has exactly one client: the Express API, using the
// Admin SDK. The Admin SDK is a server-side credential and BYPASSES security
// rules entirely, so nothing in this file can protect the data during normal
// operation — it is not in the trust path.
//
// So why have the file at all? Because "there are no rules" and "deny
// everything" behave very differently the moment something goes wrong:
//
//   - With no rules file, Firestore's default is ALLOW. A web client — or any
//     code that ends up holding a Firebase web config — could read the whole
//     `registrations` collection. That collection holds every student's name,
//     email, phone number, branch and social links. There is no way to
//     un-leak that.
//
//   - With this file, the default is DENY. A leaked Firebase web config is
//     worthless on its own. An attacker would need the service account key,
//     which lives only on the server.
//
// The Firestore emulator loads these rules, so `npm test` runs against the same
// posture as production rather than against a permissive default.
//
// IF YOU EVER NEED BROWSER ACCESS: do not weaken this file to accommodate it.
// The roster alone is 720 records of personal data, and `/api/lookup/:rollNumber`
// already returns a student's full details to anyone who knows their roll
// number. Adding a second, rules-based path to that data doubles the exposure
// instead of reducing it. If a frontend genuinely needs direct reads, put them
// behind a proxy you control and keep this file closed.
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
